    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d470">

                <pb facs="0834" n="714" xml:id="pg714"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/4419: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Schurman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-06-05T17:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">June 5, 1922—5
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received June 5—9:45 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>246. Li Yuan-hung replies to Kao En-hung<anchor n="48" xml:id="d470-f1"/> Cabinet
                    that he will resume Presidency if Generals Wu Pei-fu and Tsao Kun will agree to
                    troops’ disbandment and abolition everywhere post of Military Governor. Same
                    delegate Kao En-hung proceeding to Paotingfu this morning to present these
                    conditions which it is assumed will be accepted.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Schurman</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
